  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nTuesday - Wednesday Afternoon...\n\nThe upper pattern will feature more of a zonal flow look as we move \ninto mid week, thanks to a flattened upper ridge over the southern \nCONUS. Overall, this upper flow pattern looks to be void of any \nnotable shortwave activity, and with high pressure in place at the \nsurface this should be a dry stretch for us. Should see a good \namount of sunshine on Tuesday, however temperatures will be overall \ncooler from the recent pattern, generally ranging between 85 to 90 \ndegrees. Lows will also feel more comfortable in the mid and upper \n60s. \n\nThe bulk of daylight hours on Wednesday should be dry, however we'll \nhave to focus on timing of some shower and storm chances approaching \nfrom the NW as a weak frontal boundary trailing from an eastern \nCanada system drags toward the Ohio River Valley. We'll continue to \ncarry some shower/storm chances across southern IN by Wednesday \nafternoon to account for this. Ahead of any showers or storms, temps \nare expected to warm to the upper 80s and low 90s across central and \nsouthern KY. Southern IN and northern KY will likely be confined \nmore to the mid and upper 80s.\n\nWednesday Evening - Saturday...\n\nCould have a fairly interesting setup for later Wednesday evening \ninto Thursday as the aforementioned front likely makes its way down, \nor close to our region. In addition, models generally agree this \ntiming could be accompanied by the arrival of a shortwave embedded \nin the digging eastern CONUS trough. The two features could be the \ncatalyst for fairly good coverage of showers and storms later \nWednesday evening into Thursday. It does appear that some decent \ndeep layer shear will be present thanks to the shortwave \nenhancement, especially across our northern CWA. Tough to tell \nwhether it will overlap with the best instability, as placement of \nthe surface front could push things too far south to line up with \nthe best shear. Bottom line, there could be a chance for some \nstronger storms here, but too early to tell given uncertainty in \nexact placement of features.\n\nTroughing across the eastern CONUS downstream of amplified upper \nridging across the western CONUS will be the pattern going into the \nweekend. A couple of shortwaves embedded in the parent trough could \ncontinue to rotate through the region going through Friday and \nSaturday, potentially triggering additional showers and storms. This \nwill also keep temps a bit below normal for this time of year in the \nlow and mid 80s.\n\nSaturday Night - Sunday...\n\nLooks like the upper trough axis might push east by Sunday, leading \nto a drier pattern to end the weekend. Temps will likely continue to \nrun just below normal in the low and mid 80s.",
